window.docContents[69].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Euclidean___-polar___-and-spherical-coordinates":{"contents":"PDE arguments repeatedly move between three coordinate languages. Evolution\nequations use a canonical point of  with time stored in\ncoordinate zero. Radial differential identities replace\n by a one-variable profile. Polar integration\nreplaces Euclidean volume by an angular measure and a radial measure carrying\nthe Jacobian. These are different transformations with different hypotheses,\nso each has its own interface.\n\nThe spacetime convention is shared by the heat, wave, transport,\nconservation-law, Hamilton–Jacobi, and fluid equations.\nIf , then\n\n\n\nThe canonical vector therefore looks like  at the coordinate level.\nHowever, many analytic APIs curry a field as  or use the product\n. Explicit round trips convert between the\norders. In particular,\nsplitSpaceTime returns , joinSpaceTime accepts , and the\nseparate timeSpaceContinuousLinearEquiv exposes the order .\n\nFor derivatives, the central statement identifies the Euclidean Laplacian of\na radial field with\n away from the origin. The logarithmic and power\nprofiles then supply the calculations used by Laplace and Poisson fundamental\nsolutions.\n\nFor integration, the formal route is measure theoretic. A\nmeasure-preserving equivalence gives the coordinate change, and Fubini turns\nit into the familiar iterated polar formula with the Jacobian already encoded\nin the radial measure.\nThe radial Jacobian decides whether a singularity is integrable. In positive\ndimension, a shell of radius  and thickness  has volume\n. Thus, for the nonnegative radial function\n on the unit ball,\n\n\n\nThis is finite exactly when ; in that case its value is\n. At  the radial integral is\n, which diverges logarithmically. The value assigned at the\nsingle point  does not change the integral. This power count determines whether a singular fundamental solution is\nlocally integrable at its source.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coordinates and Boundary Calculus","header":"3.3.1. Euclidean, polar, and spherical coordinates","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Euclidean___-polar___-and-spherical-coordinates"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Analytic-Model-Methods--Power-series-and-analytic-model-methods--Coupled-analytic-evolution":{"contents":"Once the equation is solved for its time derivative, the remaining problem\nis convergence: the PDE recursively determines Taylor coefficients, but a\nformal series need not define a function. The analytic evolution construction\nbounds those coefficients by a convergent majorant, sums them, and identifies\nthe derivatives of the sum with the original equation. Evans develops this\nanalytic Cauchy theory in §4.6.3.\n\nLet . Suppose  is\nanalytic near , and every coefficient  and \nis analytic near . There are  and an analytic field \non the coordinate ball  such thatThe initial equality holds for every . The dimensions may\nbe zero. The time-independent coefficient version is stated separately at the\ncenter .\n\nSubtract  from the unknown. Analyticity of  and its spatial\nderivatives makes the transformed coefficients analytic and reduces the\ninitial trace to zero. A majorant bounds the recursively constructed formal\nsolution and gives a positive convergence radius. Compatibility of power\nseries with products, substitution, and differentiation proves the PDE for\nthe sum. Restoring  gives the original equation and trace. To include\nexplicit time dependence, adjoin a clock component with derivative one and\nzero initial value; analytic uniqueness identifies it with .\n\nUnder the same analytic coefficient and data hypotheses, two fields analytic\nnear  that satisfy the equation there and have initial trace \nnear  agree on a neighborhood of . This is uniqueness of analytic\ngerms at , and the neighborhood depends on the two solutions.\n\nAfter subtracting the initial function, the recurrence determines every\nTaylor coefficient of either solution. Analytic functions with the same\nTaylor series agree near their center. Adjoining the clock reduces the\nexplicitly time-dependent case to the same argument.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009Analytic Model Methods\u0009Power-series and analytic model methods","header":"9.2.1.8.1.1. Coupled analytic evolution","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Analytic-Model-Methods--Power-series-and-analytic-model-methods--Coupled-analytic-evolution"}});